The sky looks blue because molecules in the air scatter short‑wavelength (blue) sunlight more than longer‑wavelength colors, sending blue light in all directions. When the sun is high, this scattered blue light reaches our eyes from every part of the sky. At sunrise or sunset the light travels through more atmosphere, scattering away the blue and leaving reds and oranges.

